d as widely used for muscle-invasive bladder cancer and explicitly characterized as a major abdominal operation that removes the entire bladder (with nearby lymph nodes, and sometimes adjacent organs), requiring urinary diversion and at least several days of hospital stay. The community also enumerates downstream impacts such as bowel problems and changes in sexual function, framing radical cystectomy as high-impact even when clinically indicated. [Data: Entities (5885, 5902, 5901, 5889, 5890); Relationships (13401, 13402, 13397, 13399, 13365,+more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Disease biology is framed around mutations and uncontrolled cell growth producing cancer cells\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The network connects foundational cancer biology to bladder cancer through entities describing uncontrolled cell growth and genetic errors (mutations). Mutations are described as driving abnormal cells to produce many more cancer cells and avoid normal cell death, providing a mechanism-level explanation for tumor development and progression. [Data: Entities (4146, 5737); Relationships (8636, 12932)]\\n\\nThis biology is linked to treatment intent via the relationship that intravesical liquid medication helps destroy cancer cells, aligning therapeutic action (cell destruction) with the biological unit (cancer cells) emphasized elsewhere in the network. [Data: Entities (5892); Relationships (13340)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Kidney function is a key constraint for treatment and imaging safety, tied to lab monitoring and supportive care\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Kidneys are represented as central urinary organs that filter blood and produce urine, and the network highlights kidney function assessment through blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine testing, as well as broader blood chemistry profiling (CMP) to understand how well kidneys are working. This anchors clinical decision-making in measurable renal function. [Data: Entities (90, 2823, 268